=== CHAPTER SUMMARY (JSON, two-axis: main_sections × spines) ===
{
  "subject": "english",
  "stage": "secondary",
  "grade": "ix",
  "chapter_number": 2,
  "chapter_title": "Bharat Our Land (How I Taught My Grandmother to Read)",
  "main_sections": [
    {
      "section_id": "B",
      "title": "Bharat Our Land",
      "type": "poem",
      "page_range": "p.23-32",
      "page_count": 10,
      "poem_text": "The mighty Himavant is ours-\nthere's no equal anywhere on earth.\nThe generous Ganga is ours-\nwhich other river can match her grace?\nThe sacred Upanishads are ours-\nwhat scriptures else to name with them?\nThis sunny golden land is ours-\nshe's peerless, let's praise her!\n\nGallant warriors have lived here,\nmany a sage has sanctified this land.\nThe divinest music has been heard here,\nand here all auspicious things are found.\n\nHere Brahma-knowledge has taken root,\nand the Buddha preached his dhamma here.\nOf hoary antiquity is Bharat,\nshe's peerless, let's praise her! …",
      "poem_appreciation_summary": "Translated from Tamil poet Subramania Bharati's verse, 'Bharat Our Land' is a patriotic ode celebrating India's unmatched natural grandeur, spiritual heritage, and civilisational legacy. The poem unfolds in three stanzas: the first catalogues India's supreme natural and scriptural treasures — the Himavant mountains, the Ganga, and the Upanishads — using a parallel structure with hyphens to pair each treasure with a rhetorical assertion of its peerlessness. The second stanza shifts to India's human and cultural glory — gallant warriors, sanctifying sages, the divinest music, and auspicious abundance. The third grounds India in its philosophical depth: Brahma-knowledge and the Buddha's dhamma. A recurring refrain — 'she's peerless, let's praise her!' — unifies the poem and gives it a chant-like, elevated tone. The dominant device is apostrophe (India personified as 'she'), supported by allusion (Upanishads, Brahma-knowledge, Buddha's dhamma), hyperbole ('no equal anywhere on earth'), imagery (sunny golden land, gallant warriors), and symbolism. The mood is reverential and celebratory; the tone is passionate patriotism.",
      "spines": {
        "reading_for_comprehension": {
          "section_name": "Reflect and Respond + Reading for Appreciation + Check Your Understanding + Critical Reflection",
          "tasks_verbatim": [
            {
              "task_text": "Recall the lines of the National Anthem and complete the table given below with the names of the states, mountain ranges, and rivers mentioned in it.",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "Complete the table: States/Provinces | Mountain Ranges | Rivers mentioned in the National Anthem.",
                  "type": "FILL_IN",
                  "page_ref": "p.23"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "task_text": "Work in pairs. Discuss what the words 'जय हे' (Jaya he) in the last two lines of the National Anthem convey. Share your answer with your classmates and teacher.",
              "question_bank": []
            },
            {
              "task_text": "Read the underlined words in the given lines of the poem. Choose the odd one out that does not correspond with the meaning of the underlined word.",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "She's peerless, let's praise her! — odd one out from: valuable, incomparable, unmatched",
                  "type": "MCQ",
                  "options": [
                    "valuable",
                    "incomparable",
                    "unmatched"
                  ],
                  "page_ref": "p.23"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Many a sage has sanctified this land. — odd one out from: blessed, purified, applauded",
                  "type": "MCQ",
                  "options": [
                    "blessed",
                    "purified",
                    "applauded"
                  ],
                  "page_ref": "p.23"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "And here all auspicious things are found. — odd one out from: fortunate, fantastic, favourable",
                  "type": "MCQ",
                  "options": [
                    "fortunate",
                    "fantastic",
                    "favourable"
                  ],
                  "page_ref": "p.23"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Of hoary antiquity is Bharat — odd one out from: ancient times, recent past, time immemorial",
                  "type": "MCQ",
                  "options": [
                    "ancient times",
                    "recent past",
                    "time immemorial"
                  ],
                  "page_ref": "p.23"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "task_text": "Fill in the blanks by choosing appropriate words from the box given below to complete the summary of the poem. [Box words: warriors, peerless, Upanishads, Ganga, Buddha, Himavant, sages, praise, historical greatness, Brahma-knowledge]",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "The poem is a tribute to India, celebrating its natural beauty, spiritual heritage, and 1.____ . The poet praises the 2.____ , describing it as mighty and unparalleled. The 3.____ is depicted as generous and graceful, while the 4.____ are honoured as sacred and unmatched. The poem highlights the contributions of 5.____ and 6.____ who have enriched the land with their bravery and wisdom. It also acknowledges the presence of 7.____ and the teachings of 8.____ . The poet repeatedly asserts that India is 9.____ , urging everyone to 10.____ her.",
                  "type": "FILL_IN",
                  "page_ref": "p.25"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "task_text": "Complete the following features about the poem.",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "The impact on the readers (mood):",
                  "type": "SCR",
                  "page_ref": "p.25"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "The poet's attitude (tone):",
                  "type": "SCR",
                  "page_ref": "p.25"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "The rhyme scheme:",
                  "type": "SCR",
                  "page_ref": "p.25"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Examples of personification:",
                  "type": "SCR",
                  "page_ref": "p.25"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "task_text": "What is the impact of the refrain, 'she's peerless, let's praise her!'?",
              "question_bank": []
            },
            {
              "task_text": "Complete the following sentence appropriately. India is metaphorically described as 'this sunny golden land,' suggesting that it is ________.",
              "question_bank": []
            },
            {
              "task_text": "The poet uses symbolism. Match the symbols in Column 1 to what they suggest in Column 2. [Column 1: Himavant, Ganga, Upanishads, Gallant warriors and sages, Brahma-knowledge and Buddha's Dhamma, Sunny golden land; Column 2: knowledge and enlightenment, spiritual and philosophical legacy, purity and generosity, richness and glory, strength and permanence, courage and wisdom]",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "Himavant",
                  "type": "MATCH",
                  "page_ref": "p.26"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Ganga",
                  "type": "MATCH",
                  "page_ref": "p.26"
                },
                {
